@node Executable path
@section Executable path

@vindex goldendict-ng-executable
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-executable} specifies the path to the GoldenDict-NG binary. It accepts a file path as a string. The default value is @code{"goldendict-ng"}, which assumes the executable is on your @code{PATH}.

On macOS, GoldenDict-NG is typically installed as an application bundle, so you will usually need to set this to the full path inside the bundle:

@lisp
(setq goldendict-ng-executable
      "/Applications/GoldenDict.app/Contents/MacOS/GoldenDict")
@end lisp

If the executable cannot be found at the specified path when you invoke @code{goldendict-ng-search}, the command signals an error with installation instructions.

@node Dictionary groups
@section Dictionary groups

GoldenDict-NG organizes dictionaries into groups. The options in this section control how the package selects which group to search.

@menu
* @code{goldendict-ng-groups}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-groups-enforce}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-show-all-group}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection}::
@end menu

@node @code{goldendict-ng-groups}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-groups}

@vindex goldendict-ng-groups
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-groups} specifies an association list mapping dictionary group names to their language codes. Each element is a cons cell whose car is the group name (a string matching the group name in GoldenDict-NG) and whose cdr is an @uref{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_ISO_639_language_codes, ISO 639 language code} (a string such as @code{"en"} or @code{"fr"}). The default value is @code{'()} (an empty list).

When this list is non-empty, @code{goldendict-ng-search} prompts you to select a group before performing the search. The selected group is passed to GoldenDict-NG via its @samp{--group-name} flag. When the list is empty, no group selection occurs and GoldenDict-NG uses its own default behavior.

The language codes are used by the language detection feature (@ref{Language detection}) to automatically narrow the group candidates.

Example configuration for a multilingual setup:

@lisp
(setq goldendict-ng-groups
      '(("English dictionaries" . "en")
        ("English encyclopedias" . "en")
        ("French dictionaries" . "fr")
        ("French encyclopedias" . "fr")))
@end lisp

@node @code{goldendict-ng-groups-enforce}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-groups-enforce}

@vindex goldendict-ng-groups-enforce
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-groups-enforce} controls whether the group selection prompt requires you to choose from the listed candidates. When set to non-nil, @code{completing-read} uses the @code{REQUIRE-MATCH} argument, preventing you from entering an arbitrary group name. The default value is @code{nil}, which allows free-form input.

Set this to @code{t} if you want to ensure that only your configured groups (and optionally the ``All'' group) can be selected. This option has no effect when @code{goldendict-ng-groups} is empty.

@node @code{goldendict-ng-show-all-group}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-show-all-group}

@vindex goldendict-ng-show-all-group
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-show-all-group} controls whether the special ``All'' group appears in the group selection prompt. GoldenDict-NG's ``All'' group searches across every dictionary in your database. When set to non-nil, ``All'' is prepended to the list of candidates derived from @code{goldendict-ng-groups} (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-groups}}). The default value is @code{t}.

Set this to @code{nil} if you always want to search within a specific group and never want the option to search all dictionaries at once. This option has no effect when @code{goldendict-ng-groups} is empty.

@node @code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate}

@vindex goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate} controls whether the group selection prompt is bypassed when there is only one candidate. When set to non-nil and only one group matches (after language-based filtering, if enabled), that group is selected automatically without prompting. The default value is @code{nil}.

This is especially useful in conjunction with @code{goldendict-ng-narrow-groups-to-matching-languages} (@ref{Language detection}): when the search string is detected as belonging to a single language and only one group is configured for that language, the search proceeds immediately.

The interaction between this option and the ``All'' group is governed by @code{goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection} (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection}}).

@node @code{goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection}

@vindex goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection} controls whether the ``All'' group counts as a candidate for the purposes of auto-selection. The default value is @code{t}.

When @code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate} is non-nil and this option is also non-nil (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate}}), auto-selection occurs only when there is truly one candidate (counting ``All''). In practice, this means that if you have one matching user group plus ``All'', you will still be prompted.

When this option is set to @code{nil}, the ``All'' group is excluded from the count. This means that if there is exactly one user group plus ``All'', the user group is selected automatically without prompting, which is typically the desired behavior for users who configure specific groups and rarely want to search all dictionaries.

This option has no effect when @code{goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate} is @code{nil}.

@node Search input
@section Search input

The options in this section control how @code{goldendict-ng-search} determines the search string. The package checks for input sources in the following order of priority: (1) the active region, (2) the word at point, (3) a minibuffer prompt.

@menu
* @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point}::
@end menu

@node @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region}

@vindex goldendict-ng-use-active-region
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region} controls whether and how the active region is used when performing a search. It accepts one of three values:

@table @asis
@item @code{nil}
Ignore the active region entirely.
@item @code{bypass-prompt}
Use the text in the active region as the search string and skip the minibuffer prompt. The search is performed immediately.
@item @code{initial-input}
Use the text in the active region as the initial input for the minibuffer prompt. You can edit the text before confirming.
@end table

The default value is @code{initial-input}.

When the major mode is @code{pdf-view-mode} (from the @samp{pdf-tools} package), the region text is obtained via @code{pdf-view-active-region-text} instead of @code{buffer-substring-no-properties}.

This option takes precedence over @code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point} (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point}}) when both are non-nil and a region is active.

@node @code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point}

@vindex goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point} controls whether and how the word at point is used when performing a search. It accepts the same three values as @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region}: @code{nil}, @code{bypass-prompt}, or @code{initial-input}. The default value is @code{initial-input}.

When set to @code{bypass-prompt} and point is on a word, the search is performed immediately using that word without any prompting. When set to @code{initial-input}, the word at point is placed in the minibuffer prompt as editable initial input.

If @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region} is non-nil and a region is currently active, the region takes precedence over this option (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region}}).

@node GoldenDict-NG display flags
@section GoldenDict-NG display flags

These options correspond directly to GoldenDict-NG's command-line flags and control how the application displays results.

@menu
* @code{goldendict-ng-main-window}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-scanpopup}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-reset-window-state}::
* @code{goldendict-ng-no-tts}::
@end menu

@node @code{goldendict-ng-main-window}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-main-window}

@vindex goldendict-ng-main-window
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-main-window} controls whether the search results are forced to appear in GoldenDict-NG's main window. When set to non-nil, the @samp{--main-window} flag is passed to the executable. The default value is @code{nil}.

@node @code{goldendict-ng-scanpopup}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-scanpopup}

@vindex goldendict-ng-scanpopup
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-scanpopup} controls whether the search results are forced to appear in GoldenDict-NG's scanpopup (a floating tooltip-like window). When set to non-nil, the @samp{--scanpopup} flag is passed to the executable. The default value is @code{nil}.

@node @code{goldendict-ng-reset-window-state}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-reset-window-state}

@vindex goldendict-ng-reset-window-state
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-reset-window-state} controls whether GoldenDict-NG's window state is reset when performing a search. When set to non-nil, the @samp{--reset-window-state} flag is passed to the executable. The default value is @code{nil}.

@node @code{goldendict-ng-no-tts}
@subsection @code{goldendict-ng-no-tts}

@vindex goldendict-ng-no-tts
The user option @code{goldendict-ng-no-tts} controls whether text-to-speech is disabled for the search. When set to non-nil, the @samp{--no-tts} flag is passed to the executable. The default value is @code{nil}.

Set this to @code{t} if GoldenDict-NG's automatic pronunciation is disruptive during your workflow.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@menu
* Searching a dictionary::
@end menu

@node Searching a dictionary
@section Searching a dictionary

@findex goldendict-ng-search
The command @code{goldendict-ng-search} is the package's sole interactive entry point. Invoke it with @samp{M-x goldendict-ng-search} or bind it to a convenient key:

@lisp
(keymap-global-set "M-s d" #'goldendict-ng-search)
@end lisp

The command determines the search string using the following logic:

@enumerate
@item
If the region is active and @code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region} is set to @code{bypass-prompt} (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-use-active-region}}), the region text is used immediately.
@item
Otherwise, if point is on a word and @code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point} is set to @code{bypass-prompt} (@ref{@code{goldendict-ng-use-word-at-point}}), the word at point is used immediately.
@item
Otherwise, a minibuffer prompt appears. If a region or word at point is available and the corresponding option is set to @code{initial-input}, that text is pre-filled in the prompt.
@end enumerate

After obtaining the search string, the command optionally prompts for a dictionary group (@ref{Dictionary groups}) if @code{goldendict-ng-groups} is non-empty. It then launches GoldenDict-NG asynchronously as a subprocess with the appropriate arguments.

If the GoldenDict-NG executable cannot be found, the command signals a user error with a link to the installation page.

