@node Programmatic search
@section Programmatic search

@findex goldendict-ng-search-string
The function @code{goldendict-ng-search-string} performs a GoldenDict-NG search for a given string. It validates that the string is non-empty, builds the argument list via @code{goldendict-ng-build-args} (@ref{Argument building}), and launches the subprocess. This function is useful for integrating GoldenDict-NG searches into custom workflows or other packages.

@lisp
(goldendict-ng-search-string "serendipity")
@end lisp

@node Argument building
@section Argument building

@findex goldendict-ng-build-args
The function @code{goldendict-ng-build-args} constructs the list of command-line arguments for a search. It takes the search string and returns a list that includes the string itself, any @samp{--group-name} flag (if groups are configured), and the display flags (@code{--main-window}, @code{--scanpopup}, @code{--reset-window-state}, @code{--no-tts}) according to the current user option values.

This function is useful if you want to inspect or modify the arguments before launching the process yourself.

@node Language detection
@section Language detection

@findex goldendict-ng-get-matching-groups
@findex goldendict-ng-get-matching-languages
@findex goldendict-ng-word-is-in-language-p
@findex goldendict-ng-string-is-in-language-p
When @code{goldendict-ng-narrow-groups-to-matching-languages} is non-nil, the package uses GNU Aspell to detect which languages appear in the search string. It then narrows the group selection candidates to only those groups whose language matches.

The detection works as follows: for each unique language in @code{goldendict-ng-groups}, the function @code{goldendict-ng-string-is-in-language-p} checks whether every word in the search string is recognized by Aspell for that language. A word is considered to belong to a language if Aspell does not list it as misspelled (checked by @code{goldendict-ng-word-is-in-language-p}). The function @code{goldendict-ng-get-matching-groups} collects all groups whose language passes this test.

Note that this approach works best for single-language search strings. A multi-word string must have every word recognized in a given language for that language to match.

@node Sample configuration
@chapter Sample configuration

Here is a comprehensive example showing all user options:

@lisp
(use-package goldendict-ng
  :ensure (goldendict-ng
           :host github
           :repo "benthamite/goldendict-ng")
  :bind ("M-s d" . goldendict-ng-search)
  :config
  ;; Set the path to the executable (required on macOS)
  (setq goldendict-ng-executable
        "/Applications/GoldenDict.app/Contents/MacOS/GoldenDict")

  ;; Configure dictionary groups with their languages
  (setq goldendict-ng-groups
        '(("English dictionaries" . "en")
          ("English encyclopedias" . "en")
          ("French dictionaries" . "fr")
          ("French encyclopedias" . "fr")))

  ;; Narrow groups based on language detection
  (setq goldendict-ng-narrow-groups-to-matching-languages t)

  ;; Auto-select when only one group matches
  (setq goldendict-ng-auto-select-sole-candidate t)
  (setq goldendict-ng-count-all-group-in-auto-selection nil))
@end lisp

